Problem 5 – Ride-Sharing Algorithm
Problem

The system must match riders and drivers quickly despite
100,000+ requests per minute.

Bottlenecks
Linear search through all drivers
Repeated database queries
Lock contention
Network latency
Recalculating distances
Large amounts of unnecessary data
Centralized processing
Optimized Approach

A geospatial index should be used instead of scanning every driver.

Rider Request
     |
     v
Location Service
     |
     v
Geospatial Index
     |
     v
Nearby Drivers
     |
     v
Filter Drivers
     |
     v
Calculate ETA
     |
     v
Rank Candidates
     |
     v
Reserve Driver
     |
     v
Return Match
Data Structures

Instead of:

for every driver:
    calculate distance

use a spatial structure such as:

Geohash
R-tree
QuadTree
Spatial database index

This reduces the number of drivers that must be examined.

Caching

Frequently used information can be stored in Redis:

Redis
 |
 +-- Driver location
 +-- Driver availability
 +-- Vehicle type
 +-- Current status

This avoids unnecessary database requests.

Concurrency Model

The system can use:

Asynchronous I/O
Worker processes
Message queues
Multiple compute nodes
Partitioned geographic regions
Distributed Architecture
                    Rider Requests
                          |
                          v
                 +----------------+
                 | Load Balancer  |
                 +-------+--------+
                         |
          +--------------+--------------+
          |              |              |
          v              v              v
     +---------+    +---------+    +---------+
     | Node 1  |    | Node 2  |    | Node 3  |
     | Chennai |    | Mumbai  |    | Delhi   |
     +----+----+    +----+----+    +----+----+
          |              |              |
          v              v              v
       Redis          Redis          Redis
          |              |              |
          +--------------+--------------+
