1. Explain technical debt. What are the major causes of technical debt, and how can organizations minimize it during software evolution?
Definition of Technical Debt

Technical debt refers to the future cost or additional effort created when a software team chooses a quick, easy, or temporary solution instead of a better long-term solution.

Just like financial debt, technical debt may help a project move faster initially, but it creates interest in the form of increased maintenance, bugs, complexity, and development time later.

Example

Suppose a developer needs to implement a feature quickly and writes duplicated code instead of creating reusable functions.

Initially:

Feature completed quickly
        ↓
Faster release

Later:

Duplicated code
      ↓
More maintenance
      ↓
More bugs
      ↓
Slower development
      ↓
Higher cost

Therefore, technical debt is not necessarily bad. Some technical debt can be intentional, especially when a business needs an early release. The problem occurs when debt is ignored or continuously accumulated.

Major Causes of Technical Debt
1. Tight deadlines

Developers may choose shortcuts to meet release deadlines.

Example: Implementing a simple hard-coded solution instead of designing a scalable architecture.

2. Poor software design

Incorrect architecture, excessive coupling, and poor separation of responsibilities make future modifications difficult.

3. Lack of testing

When unit, integration, and automated tests are missing, bugs accumulate and future changes become risky.

4. Outdated technologies

Old frameworks, libraries, and dependencies may become difficult to maintain or vulnerable to security problems.

5. Inadequate documentation

If the original developers leave without documenting the system, future developers may struggle to understand the code.

6. Poor code quality

Examples include:

Duplicate code
Very large functions
Unclear variable names
Hard-coded values
Unnecessary complexity
7. Frequent requirement changes

Continuous changes in business requirements can cause temporary implementations that later need restructuring.

8. Lack of code reviews

Without peer review, poor design and coding practices can enter the production codebase.

How Organizations Can Minimize Technical Debt
1. Perform regular code reviews

Every important change should be reviewed by other developers.

Developer
    ↓
Pull Request
    ↓
Code Review
    ↓
Automated Tests
    ↓
Merge

This helps identify design and quality problems early.

2. Use automated testing

Organizations should maintain:

Unit tests
Integration tests
API tests
End-to-end tests

Automated testing reduces the risk of introducing new defects.

3. Refactor regularly

Developers should periodically improve existing code without changing its functionality.

Examples:

Remove duplicate code
Improve class/function design
Simplify complex logic
Update dependencies
Improve database queries
4. Maintain technical-debt backlog

Technical debt should be recorded as engineering tasks and prioritized along with new features.

Example:

Sprint:
70% New Features
20% Bug Fixes
10% Technical Debt

The exact percentage can vary depending on the project.

5. Keep dependencies updated

Regularly updating libraries and frameworks helps improve:

Security
Performance
Compatibility
Maintainability
6. Follow coding standards

Using coding guidelines, linters, static analysis, and architectural standards prevents poor-quality code from accumulating.

7. Maintain documentation

Architecture diagrams, API documentation, setup instructions, and design decisions should be maintained as the software evolves.

Conclusion

Technical debt is the accumulated cost of choosing short-term solutions that make future development more difficult. It can be minimized through code reviews, automated testing, refactoring, documentation, dependency management, coding standards, and continuous monitoring. Managing technical debt throughout software evolution results in a more maintainable, secure, scalable, and reliable system.

