Predictive maintenance
B. Edge Computing
Definition

Edge computing processes data close to where it is generated instead of sending all data to a centralized cloud server.

Traditional cloud architecture:

Device
   ↓
Internet
   ↓
Cloud Server
   ↓
Response

Edge architecture:

Device
   ↓
Edge Server
   ↓
Immediate Response

        ↓
      Cloud
(for storage/large-scale processing)
How Edge Computing Is Transforming Software Engineering
1. Lower latency

Data can be processed close to the user or device, reducing network delay.

2. Reduced bandwidth usage

Not every piece of data needs to be sent to the cloud.

3. Improved reliability

Applications can continue operating even when connectivity to the central cloud is temporarily unavailable.

4. Better real-time processing

Edge computing is useful for applications requiring immediate decisions.

Examples include:

Autonomous vehicles
Industrial robots
Smart cameras
Healthcare monitoring
Smart factories
IoT systems
Practical Example of Edge Computing

Smart traffic management

Traffic cameras generate large amounts of video data.

Instead of sending every video frame to the cloud:

Traffic Camera
      ↓
Edge Computer
      ↓
Detect vehicles/congestion
      ↓
Traffic signal decision
      ↓
Cloud
(analytics + historical storage)

The edge device can detect traffic congestion locally and immediately adjust traffic signals.

Benefits
Faster response
Lower bandwidth requirements
Reduced cloud processing cost
Better real-time decision-making
AI/ML vs Edge Computing
Feature	AI/ML	Edge Computing
Main purpose	Intelligent prediction/automation	Local data processing
Main benefit	Intelligent decisions	Low latency
Common use	Recommendation systems	IoT/real-time systems
Processing	Cloud, local, or distributed	Near the data source
Example	Product recommendation	Smart traffic control
Conclusion

AI/ML makes software more intelligent and automated, while Edge Computing makes applications more responsive and efficient by processing data closer to its source. Together, they enable modern applications such as autonomous systems, smart cities, intelligent manufacturing, and personalized digital services.

3. Case Study – Sustainable DevOps Practices
Scenario

A company deploys dozens of microservices in the cloud. Resource usage and energy consumption are increasing.

The company should adopt Sustainable DevOps, which combines DevOps automation and operational efficiency with environmental sustainability.

Three important practices are described below.

Practice 1: Right-Sizing and Autoscaling

The company should avoid running unnecessarily large servers or excessive numbers of containers.

Problem

Suppose a microservice requires:

Normal traffic → 2 Pods
Peak traffic   → 10 Pods

If 10 Pods are running all day:

Low traffic
    ↓
10 Pods running
    ↓
Unused resources
    ↓
Higher cost + energy consumption
Solution

Use Kubernetes Horizontal Pod Autoscaling (HPA) or cloud autoscaling.

Low Traffic
    ↓
2 Pods

High Traffic
    ↓
10 Pods

Traffic decreases
    ↓
2 Pods

Similarly, CPU and memory resources should be right-sized according to actual usage.

Benefits
Lower cloud cost
Lower CPU and memory consumption
Reduced energy consumption
Better resource utilization
Practice 2: Efficient CI/CD and Infrastructure Automation

CI/CD pipelines can consume significant computing resources if every build unnecessarily performs expensive operations.

The company can optimize pipelines by:

Running only relevant tests
Caching dependencies
Reusing Docker layers
Using lightweight build agents
Automatically shutting down temporary environments
Scheduling non-urgent workloads efficiently
Removing unused cloud resources
Example

Instead of rebuilding everything:

Every commit
     ↓
Build entire application
     ↓
Run all jobs
     ↓
High compute usage

An optimized pipeline can use:

Commit
  ↓
Identify changed service
  ↓
Build only required service
  ↓
Run relevant tests
  ↓
Deploy
Benefits
Fewer CPU cycles
Faster builds
Lower CI/CD cost
Reduced energy consumption
Practice 3: Container and Cloud Resource Optimization

Microservices should use lightweight containers and efficient infrastructure.

Techniques
Use lightweight Docker images

Instead of very large base images, use smaller production images where appropriate.

Example:

Large image
   ↓
More storage + network transfer

Small image
   ↓
Less storage + faster deployment
Remove unused resources

Organizations should regularly identify and remove:

Unused virtual machines
Unused containers
Unused storage
Idle load balancers
Old snapshots
Unused development environments
Use serverless or managed services where appropriate

For workloads that run only occasionally, serverless computing can avoid keeping servers running continuously.

Monitor resource utilization

Use cloud monitoring to track:

CPU
Memory
Storage
Network
Pod utilization
Request rate

Resources can then be adjusted according to actual demand.

Sustainable DevOps Architecture
                  Cloud Platform
                       │
              ┌────────┴────────┐
              │                 │
         Microservices      Monitoring
              │                 │
              ▼                 ▼
        Kubernetes          Metrics
              │                 │
              ▼                 │
          Autoscaling ◄─────────┘
              │
              ▼
       Right-sized Pods
              │
              ▼
       Lower Resource Use
              │
        ┌─────┴─────┐
        ▼           ▼
   Lower Cost    Less Energy
Summary of the Three Practices
Sustainable DevOps Practice	How It Helps	Result
Right-sizing + Autoscaling	Runs only required resources	Lower cloud cost and energy
Efficient CI/CD	Avoids unnecessary builds and compute	Faster pipelines and lower consumption
Container/Cloud Optimization	Uses efficient images and removes idle resources	Better utilization and reduced waste
Conclusion

Sustainable DevOps helps organizations reduce both cloud expenditure and environmental impact. By using autoscaling and right-sized infrastructure, optimizing CI/CD pipelines, and eliminating unnecessary cloud resources, the company can operate its microservices more efficiently. Continuous monitoring is important because resource optimization should be based on actual workload patterns rather than assumptions.
